#### Issue #7: JSX: Reactive props (class, innerHTML) not automatically tracked

# Luna JSX: Reactive Props Not Tracked

## Summary

Luna JSX doesn't automatically track reactive values in props like `class` and `innerHTML`. Users must manually use `ref` + `createEffect` for DOM property updates, which is verbose and error-prone.

## Reproduction

```tsx
import { render, createSignal, createMemo } from "@luna_ui/luna";

function App() {
  const [active, setActive] = createSignal(false);
  const className = createMemo(() => `btn ${active() ? "active" : ""}`);

  return (
    <div>
      {/* ❌ Does NOT work - class never updates */}
      <button class={className()} onClick={() => setActive(v => !v)}>
        Toggle
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

render(document.getElementById("app")!, <App />);
```

**Expected**: Clicking the button toggles the `active` class
**Actual**: Class is set once on mount, never updates

## Current Workaround

```tsx
function App() {
  const [active, setActive] = createSignal(false);
  const className = createMemo(() => `btn ${active() ? "active" : ""}`);
  let btnRef: HTMLButtonElement | null = null;

  // Manual effect for each reactive prop
  createEffect(() => {
    if (btnRef) btnRef.className = className();
  });

  return (
    <button ref={(el) => { btnRef = el; }} onClick={() => setActive(v => !v)}>
      Toggle
    </button>
  );
}
```

This workaround is:
- Verbose (3x more code)
- Error-prone (easy to forget the effect)
- Doesn't scale (need ref + effect for every reactive prop)

## Same Issue with `innerHTML`

```tsx
// ❌ Does NOT work - sets literal attribute innerHTML="<svg>..."
<span innerHTML={svgString} />

// ✅ Workaround
function Icon(props: { svg: string }) {
  let ref: HTMLSpanElement | null = null;
  createEffect(() => {
    if (ref) ref.innerHTML = props.svg;
  });
  return <span ref={(el) => { ref = el; }} />;
}
```

## Expected Behavior (SolidJS Reference)

In SolidJS (which Luna is inspired by), reactive props work automatically:

```tsx
// SolidJS - this just works
<button class={className()}>Toggle</button>
<span innerHTML={svgString} />
```

SolidJS achieves this through compile-time transformations that wrap reactive expressions in effects.

## Proposed Solutions

### Option 1: Compile-time Transformation (Recommended)

Add a Babel/SWC plugin that transforms JSX:

```tsx
// Input
<div class={className()} />

// Output
const _el = document.createElement("div");
createEffect(() => _el.className = className());
```

### Option 2: Runtime Detection

Detect if a prop value is a function (accessor) and wrap it in an effect:

```tsx
// In Luna's JSX runtime
function setProp(el, key, value) {
  if (typeof value === 'function' && isReactiveProp(key)) {
    createEffect(() => el[key] = value());
  } else {
    el[key] = value;
  }
}
```

### Option 3: Special Prop Syntax

Introduce explicit reactive prop syntax:

```tsx
// Use $ prefix for reactive props
<div $class={className} />  // Pass accessor, not called
<span $innerHTML={svgSignal} />
```

## Impact

This issue affects all dynamic styling patterns:
- Conditional classes (`class={condition() ? "a" : "b"}`)
- Dynamic styles (`style={{ color: colorSignal() }}`)
- innerHTML for SVG icons, sanitized HTML, etc.

## Environment

- Luna version: 0.3.3
- Discovered while building: markdown editor with syntax highlighting
- Test file: `playground/luna.test.tsx` (confirms memo/effect work correctly, issue is JSX binding)

## Related

- SolidJS reactive props: https://www.solidjs.com/docs/latest/api#props
- SolidJS innerHTML: https://www.solidjs.com/docs/latest/api#innerhtml

