#### Issue #35797: node:crypto Cipheriv/Decipheriv final(encoding) drops StringDecoder remainder: base64 output silently truncated for stream ciphers (GCM/CTR)

_Notice: Used Fable 5 heavily to find out about this issue, suggested fix and other notes._

---

Version: Deno 2.9.1 (also reproduced on 2.9.0; affects every release since v2.5.2)

`node:crypto` Cipheriv/Decipheriv silently truncate their output when a string output encoding is used with a stream-mode cipher (AES-GCM, AES-CTR, ChaCha20-Poly1305, …): `final(encoding)` returns `""` without flushing the `StringDecoder` that `update()` used, so the up-to-2 bytes the base64 decoder buffered are dropped. The result is corrupted ciphertext whenever the input byte length is not a multiple of 3 — with no error thrown.

## Repro

```js
import { createCipheriv, createDecipheriv } from "node:crypto";

const key = Buffer.alloc(32, 1), iv = Buffer.alloc(12, 2);

const c = createCipheriv("aes-256-gcm", key, iv);
const out = c.update("hello", "utf8", "base64") + c.final("base64");
console.log(out);
// Deno 2.9.1: "b7Ol"      (3 bytes — TRUNCATED)
// Node 26.4:  "b7OlJSU="  (5 bytes — correct)
```

Decipheriv has the same bug on its string-output path:

```js
const d = createDecipheriv("aes-256-gcm", key, iv);
d.setAuthTag(tag); // tag from a correct 5-byte encryption of "hello"
const dec = d.update(fullCiphertext, undefined, "base64") + d.final("base64");
// Deno: "aGVs"      (3 bytes of plaintext)
// Node: "aGVsbG8="  (5 bytes)
```

The Buffer API is unaffected: `Buffer.concat([c.update("hello", "utf8"), c.final()])` produces the correct 5 bytes.

Affected: stream-mode ciphers (`_needsBlockCache === false`) with stateful output encodings (base64; utf16le/utf8 on the decipher side — a truncated multibyte tail drops the flush-emitted U+FFFD vs Node). Not affected: CBC/ECB (the block path flushes), hex (stateless decoder), Buffer output.

## Root cause

`ext/node/polyfills/internal/crypto/cipher.ts`: `update()` routes string output through `this._decoder.write(output)`, and a base64 `StringDecoder` withholds `len % 3` trailing bytes until `end()`. But the early-return paths in `Cipheriv.prototype.final` — AES-wrap (https://github.com/denoland/deno/blob/v2.9.1/ext/node/polyfills/internal/crypto/cipher.ts#L231-L234), the no-block-cache/stream-cipher path (https://github.com/denoland/deno/blob/v2.9.1/ext/node/polyfills/internal/crypto/cipher.ts#L244-L249), and the auth-tag path (https://github.com/denoland/deno/blob/v2.9.1/ext/node/polyfills/internal/crypto/cipher.ts#L266-L270) — all do

```js
return encoding === "buffer" ? Buffer.from([]) : "";
```

without calling `this._decoder.end()`. Only the CBC/ECB block path flushes (L274). `Decipheriv.prototype.final` mirrors the same unflushed early returns. Node's implementation always returns `this._decoder.end(ret)` when an output encoding is set.

The bug was introduced by #30806 (the fix for #30722), first shipped in v2.5.2, and is still present on main — later cipher fixes (#31389, #31533, #32403, #33079) don't touch these paths.

## Suggested fix

Make every `final()` return path flush the decoder, e.g. replace the early returns with

```js
return encoding === "buffer" ? Buffer.from([]) : this._decoder!.end();
```

(after ensuring `_lazyInitCipherDecoder`/`_lazyInitDecipherDecoder` ran for that path), in both `Cipheriv.final` and `Decipheriv.final`. `StringDecoder.end()` emits the buffered remainder with correct base64 padding, matching Node.

## Real-world impact

This corrupted AES-256-GCM-encrypted financial data in our production database after migrating a Bun app to Deno: every encrypted field whose plaintext length % 3 != 0 was stored truncated and later failed decryption with `Unsupported state or unable to authenticate data` (the GCM tag covers the full ciphertext). Because encryption "succeeds", there is no signal until the data is read back. Recovery required brute-forcing the dropped bytes against the stored auth tag.

