#### Issue #467: validating raw Json against Json schema generated from C# interfaces

I'm using NJsonSchema to validate raw Json strings against a JsonSchema generated from a .Net type.  The .Net type is an interface containing a child property that's also an interface.  

Here's my interface:

    public interface IPersonDataContract
	{
		Int32 PersonId { get; set; }
		String FirstName { get; set; }
		String LastName { get; set; }
		String Name { get; set; }
		DateTime? Birthday { get; set; }
		Int32 Age { get; }
		IEnumerable<IPhoneNumberDataContract> PhoneNumberList { get; set; }
	}

Here's the interface describing an item in the PhoneNumberList property:

    public interface IPhoneNumberDataContract
	{
		Int32 PhoneNumberTypeId { get; set; }
		String PhoneNumberTypeName { get; set; }
		String PhoneNumber { get; set; }
	}

I'm using NJsonSchema to validate raw Json serialized from a PersonDataContract instance (PersonDataContract implements IPersonDataContract) against the JsonSchema generated from IPersonDataContract.  I'm receiving validation errors because PhoneNumberList is defined as an interface (IEnumerable of IPhoneNumberDataContract).  If I change the definition of PhoneNumberList to a concrete class (IEnumerable of PhoneNumberDataContract), then no errors are produced through validation.

Here's the JsonSchema generated from IPersonDataContract with IPhoneDataContract:

    
	{
		"$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#",
		"title": "IListOfPersonDataContract",
		"type": "array",
		"items": {
